# Twilight Syndrome: Tansaku-hen (SLPS-00102): 16-bit little-endian codes (01 00 = あ), keys in memory byte order.
# This file is what every chapter shares: 0x0000 is a space and 0x0001-0x0108 the kana, digits, Latin letters and
# punctuation. Kanji are 0x010C up and numbered anew by each chapter: one of SLPS-00102-ch0.tbl, -ch1, -ch2, -ch3,
# -ch4 or -chX goes after this file (named after the disc folders DAT/CAP0 ... DAT/CAPX that hold the scripts).
# Which byte in memory says the chapter isn't known yet. Not Kyuumei-hen's codes (SLPS-00405), though the engine
# and the command words are the same.
# Worked out from the disc: the font file's order, and every scene's script decoded and checked against a word
# list. Then confirmed in play: fourteen Genkan snapshots of the first chapter (seven different scripts) decode
# with no unmapped code, to the lines that were on screen.
# Placed by their block's order only, since no scene uses them: ｚ (0x00EF), ￥ (0x00F9), ＝ (0x00FA), … (0x00FC),
# ’ (0x00FF). The game writes ・・・ for an ellipsis. Not mapped: 0x0109-0x010B (icons, by Kyuumei-hen's layout;
# 0x010A is used twice).
# Words from 0x8000 up are commands: 0x8100 new line, 0x8500 wait for a press with the text going on below
# (the window scrolls), 0x8600 wait for a press at a page's end (the window is cleared), 0x8D0n a speaker's page
# (a name of n codes follows, then ：), 0x84nn indent, 0xFFFF the script's end; the rest drive answers, pauses
# and pictures. 246 different ones are used. The meanings of the two waits are Kyuumei-hen's, seen in a replay
# of that game; here the snapshots only show the game stopped just past each kind.
# The running script is at 0x801FE800, and the u16 at 0x800A4CEA is the index of the next word the game will read:
# just past the wait code while it waits for a press, and just past 0xFFFF once the script is over. Beside it:
# the u16 at 0x800A4CE4 is 0xFFFE while the game waits for a press and 0 otherwise; the u16 at 0x800A4CEE counts
# characters typed and is 0 when no text is shown; the byte at 0x800A4D12 is 1 in a conversation's window but 0
# in the opening's narration, so it doesn't say "text is shown". The bytes at 0x800A4D10 are 01 FF at a 0x8500
# wait and FF 01 at a 0x8600 one.
# All of that from the first chapter only (the fourteen snapshots: ten waits, two while typing, two with no
# window). Kyuumei-hen moves these addresses from chapter to chapter, so the other chapters' may differ.
